// SL:borrowing §3: two READ-ONLY borrows through an accessor declared
// `&var self`, on ONE `var` root, are two EXCLUSIVE root charges and
// conflict.
//
// The root charge follows the accessor's declaration, never how a use site
// happens to use the place: an accessor that takes `&var self` holds its root
// exclusively under bare `borrow` and `borrow var` alike. Both borrows below
// only read, and each lasts for the call it is an argument of (§2.2), so `b`
// would be charged exclusively twice at once: the same conflict as
// `setboth(borrow var b.tally(0), borrow var b.tally(1))`, reached through a
// pair of shared `borrow` arguments.
//
// A read that should share the root needs a declared shared accessor, written
// or `@synthesize(shared)` (§4). `tally` cannot take the synthesized one: its
// epilogue writes `self`, so its body fails under the shared signature, and
// no use site can narrow the charge instead.
//
// The MIXED pair — one borrow through a shared twin and one through an
// accessor with none, in both orders — is `mixed_window_modes_conflict.saw`.
// Every pairing is one rule: borrows of one root that are live together in a
// statement are checked together, and an exclusive charge conflicts with any
// other access to that root (§2.2, §3).
//
// Nothing about `tally`'s epilogue enters into it: the declaration alone
// decides the charge.
// EXPECT: error

struct Bag { cells: [Int; 4], visits: Int }

extension Bag {
    public func tally(&var self, i: Int) borrows -> &var Int {
        if i < 0 || i >= 4 { panic("Bag.tally: index out of range") }
        lend self.cells[i]
        self.visits = self.visits + 1
    }
}

func pair(a: &Int, b: &Int) -> Int { a + b }

func main() {
    var b = Bag(cells: [10, 20, 30, 40], visits: 0)
    print("{pair(borrow b.tally(0), borrow b.tally(1))}")
}
